A||||8||Dan||Ice on headlights||||1||
Z||000000||Dan||12-09-1999||10:18 AM|||| With this being my first Fiero winter I was wondering if there is any problems with the headlights icing up during bad weather?||||reg||1
Z||000001||BOILERMAKER||12-09-1999||10:40 AM||||Ummmmmmmmmmmm, yeah, kinda!  How's that for definitive.<P>Last year when Indiana got that big snow on Jan 1 (I think that was when it was) I couldn't open my lights until it warmed up a little.  You want to watch the instinct to hatchet at it with the ice scraper due to other problems that could occur.  Warm water would be a better resolution, but then again a garage would be the best choice.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Voyd||12-09-1999||10:50 AM||||weve already had a few nights with frost and the car had about an inch of frost all over it yet my headlights went up perfectly everytime. dunno, have to wait and see what happens throughout the rest of winter.<p>[This message has been edited by Voyd (edited 12-09-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||12-09-1999||12:46 PM||||If you disconnect the blue (I think) wire at the headlight mechanism, the lights will not retract when turned off.  You can also patch a switch in series into the white wire at the light switch which will allow you to disarm the down control when you wish. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||william_thorniii||12-09-1999||03:11 PM||||Although I hate to say it, but the best solution is to just leave them up during the winter.  It doesn't look very good and everyone asks if you need help fixing them... but for outside cars it is not feasible to do otherwise.  Because it stinks when you have to be at work but your car is under a 1/4" thick sheet of ice and your options are to drive w/o headlights and look like a gangster, to be late or to hack, hack, hack away at them until they come up.  Although I have found that lifting the hood (if possible) often allows the lamp doors to be pushed up from the backside.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Scott2M4||12-10-1999||08:47 AM||||Let me see if I have this straight (for those of us that have "outside" cars during the winter months):<P>(1) Raise the lights with the dash switch.<BR>(2) Disconnect negative battery terminal (safety reasons).<BR>(3) Disconnect the _____-colored wire (blue on 87-88???) from the motors.<BR>(4) Reconnect the battery terminal.<BR>(5) Turn off the dash switch.<P>The light assemblies should now stay up until this wire is reconnected.  Correct?  Have I missed anything?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||12-10-1999||10:38 AM||||Although is it always safetest to disconnect the battery when working on things electrical it is not really necessary if all you want to do is unplug the blue wire.  With the headlights off and down raise the hood.  You will see the blue wire at each headlight assembly.  This wire gives the signal to lower the headlights when the lights are switched off.  Unplug the blue wire at each headlight.  Now when you turn on the lights they will go up but will stay up when turned off.<P>The reason I mentioned disconnecting the battery earlier was I thought the headlight was stuck in the up position.  You wouldn't want your hand around the headlight assembly when they are up because they could accidentally slam shut at any time.  They will not likely accidentally open on their own.  They do close with a considerable amount of force. <P>Good luck,<BR>Carroll<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||12-10-1999||01:42 PM||||Here's a better idea.  Switch motors with me.  Mine won't go up or down.  This way yours won't get clogged with ice.  (Aren't I helpful?!)||||reg||1
Z||000008||william_thorniii||12-10-1999||08:24 PM||||Monkeyman, <P>You are hilarious!  If the problem is mechanical they are easy to fix.  Let me know if you need information. <P>Scott, <P>On the 1987-1988's there are two small harnasses (spelling?) that go to the headlights.  It should be OBVIOUS which control the motor since they go right to it.  If you are not sure, I can stop by and help you out. <P>William T. Thorn, III<BR>937-259-5295 (work)<BR>513-422-2002 (home) <P>||||reg||1
